  "id": "Compound::DB00588 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Fluticasone Propionate, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in its relationship with Nr3c1 through a shared pathway involving corticosteroid receptor activation. The evidence suggests that Fluticasone Propionate, as part of the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist class, binds to Nr3c1, a gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor. This binding is supported by multiple paths, all of which point to Nr3c1 as a target of corticosteroid agonists, with Fluticasone Propionate being a representative member of this class. The lowest common ancestors across these paths—Corticosteroid Hormone Receptor Agonists—highlight the common biological function of activating Nr3c1 through corticosteroid signaling. The consistent evidence from these paths implies that Fluticasone Propionate exerts its effects by modulating Nr3c1 activity, likely through the activation of the glucocorticoid receptor pathway. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a lowest common ancestor in the corticosteroid class, which is a clinically relevant and biologically plausible pathway. The evidence does not provide direct evidence of a specific binding affinity or IC50 value, but the shared pathway and the presence of corticosteroid agonists in the class suggest a mechanistic link that is consistent with the role of Nr3c1 in corticosteroid signaling.",
